more than 3 million people have fled the  war in ukraine and that number is  growing mike armstrong has more on why  leaving or staying are both risky  options  these are images released by an aide to  the ukrainian president they are  refugees being picked up from two cities  in the central part of the country and  taken to safer areas of the ten  humanitarian corridors open on this day  two led to the city of bravery just east  of kiev  stories from that city's hospital help  explain the challenge and the danger of  trying to get around this man was shot  three times  he says he's a civilian and was  traveling in a civilian vehicle russian  soldiers he says shot through his  windshield  if moving is dangerous so is staying put  in a place like mariupol right now it  means more misery every day  this woman just escaped  this woman says she spent 10 days in a  cellar hiding from bombs and didn't  leave once they had no electricity and  no running water  in the town of zaparica a grocery store  is turned into a refugee center many of  the people in it left mariupol and  consider themselves lucky  this man says anyone who could leave did  but many didn't make it they drove by  bombed and burned out cars on their way  out of town there were bodies he says in  the streets  now this is where so many want to go  west to poland of the more than 3.2  million refugees who fled ukraine about  two-thirds have crossed into poland  this mother of five says by the time she  decided to leave her home west of keeve  with her children there was no food and  no way to stay  on this day among those crossing over  was a woman whose destination is canada  she'd been on vacation was home in  ukraine visiting her parents from  studying sociology in canada when the  war broke out she didn't regret being  there actually i'm kind of really glad  that i could go here even though it's  like it's real terrible right now but at  least i could see my parents because i  don't know it's gonna be  she says right now her future is hard to  worry about she's safe in poland her  thoughts are all about her parents who  stayed behind  mike armstrong global news czech poland
 

